# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values. Never commit .env itself.

# --- Google Cloud OAuth credentials ---
# Create these at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# 1. Create a project (or use an existing one)
# 2. Enable the "Google Calendar API" under APIs & Services > Library
# 3. Configure the OAuth consent screen (External is fine; add your own
#    Gmail as a test user while the app is unpublished)
# 4. Create OAuth client ID credentials, type "Web application"
# 5. Add this exact Authorized redirect URI:
#    http://localhost:4000/api/auth/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:4000/api/auth/callback

# Which calendar to check/write to. "primary" uses the connected account's
# main calendar. To use a specific calendar instead, use its Calendar ID
# (found in Google Calendar > Settings > Settings for my calendars > that
# calendar > Integrate calendar > Calendar ID).
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID=primary

# --- Clinic settings ---
CONSULTATION_LENGTH_MINUTES=60
CLINIC_TIMEZONE=Europe/London

# --- Server ---
PORT=4000
# The URL(s) of the React dev/build client, for CORS. Comma-separate multiple
# origins if more than one frontend needs to reach this server — e.g. your
# production Netlify site plus a Netlify branch deploy (branch deploys get
# their own origin, like https://test-branch--yoursite.netlify.app):
# CLIENT_ORIGIN=https://ladermaclinic.netlify.app,https://test-branch--ladermaclinic.netlify.app
CLIENT_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# The backend's own public URL (not the client's) — used to build the
# "Add to Calendar" link in the client's confirmation email, since that
# link needs to point at this server's /api/bookings/:id/calendar.ics
# route. Defaults to http://localhost:<PORT> if not set, which only works
# for local testing — set this to your real Render/hosting URL in
# production, e.g. https://laderma-v3.onrender.com
# PUBLIC_API_BASE=https://laderma-v3.onrender.com

# --- Data persistence on hosts with an ephemeral filesystem ---
# On most cloud hosts (Render's free tier included), the local filesystem
# is wiped on every redeploy or restart, so anything written to a local
# file — admin accounts, treatment pricing, client accounts/bookings —
# would disappear the moment the service restarts, unless it's stored
# somewhere that survives that.
#
# --- Option A: Turso (recommended — works on Render's FREE tier) ---
# admins/pricing/clients now live in a real SQL database via
# @libsql/client, which can point at either a local file (default, used
# automatically if the two variables below are unset) or a free hosted
# Turso database — the exact same code path either way, just a different
# connection string, so nothing else changes when you switch.
#
# Turso's free tier (no credit card required) is far more than this site
# will ever need: 500 million row reads/month, 10 million row writes/
# month, 5GB storage. This is what actually fixes the free-tier data-loss
# problem, since Render's free tier has no persistent disk option at all.
#
# 1. Sign up free at https://turso.tech (email or GitHub)
# 2. Create a database (any name, e.g. "laderma")
# 3. Copy its Database URL (starts with libsql://) and create an Auth
#    Token for it
# 4. Set both here (and in Render's environment variables once deployed):
# TURSO_DATABASE_URL=libsql://your-database-name.turso.io
# TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN=your-auth-token-here
#
# Leave both unset for local development — the server falls back to a
# local file (server/data/laderma.db) automatically, no Turso account
# needed to develop or test locally.
#
# --- Option B: a persistent disk (needs a PAID Render instance) ---
# If you're already on a paid plan with a disk mounted, you can instead
# point local storage at that disk with DATA_DIR — the disk add-on isn't
# available on Render's free tier, so this only helps once you've
# upgraded. Dashboard → your service → Disks → Add Disk, mount path e.g.
# /var/data, then set:
# DATA_DIR=/var/data
# This only affects the local-file fallback above — if TURSO_DATABASE_URL
# is set, DATA_DIR is ignored for the database (Turso IS the persistence),
# though it's still used for the Google Calendar token file below.

# --- Google Calendar token persistence ---
# The Google Calendar token doesn't live in the database above (it's a
# single OAuth token, not relational data), so it still uses its own
# env-var fallback: connect once (locally or on the live URL), copy the
# token JSON shown on the confirmation page into this variable, and
# redeploy. The server reads from here first, falling back to the local
# file (server/data/google-token.json, or DATA_DIR if set) otherwise.
# GOOGLE_TOKEN_JSON={"access_token":"...","refresh_token":"...","scope":"...","token_type":"Bearer","expiry_date":...}

# --- Email notifications (optional) ---
# Two separate email paths, using two separate services, because of
# genuine platform restrictions:
# - Resend's free tier only allows sending TO your own verified account
#   email until a domain is verified, so it cannot email real clients yet.
# - Raw SMTP (e.g. Gmail) is blocked outbound on Render's free tier, so
#   that isn't an option either.
# Brevo's free tier is a genuine HTTP API (not SMTP, so it isn't blocked
# by Render) and does not require domain verification to send to real
# recipients (up to 300/day free), so it's used for the client-facing
# email instead. Once you verify a domain in Resend, you can switch the
# client email over to Resend too if you'd prefer one provider for
# everything — see the note at the bottom.

# --- 1. Client confirmation email (via Brevo) ---
# The branded confirmation + "Add to Calendar" email sent to whoever
# books a consultation.
#
# 1. Sign up free at https://www.brevo.com
# 2. Go to Settings > SMTP & API > API Keys, generate a new API key
# 3. Go to Senders, Domains & Dedicated IPs > Senders, and add/verify a
#    sender email address (Brevo will email you a confirmation link —
#    this is a lightweight one-click verification, not a domain setup).
#    laderma.testing@gmail.com works fine as a sender here.
# BREVO_API_KEY=xkeysib-your-api-key-here
# BREVO_SENDER_EMAIL=laderma.testing@gmail.com

# --- 2. Clinic new-booking notification (via Resend) ---
# Sign up free at https://resend.com, create an API key under API Keys.
# This works today because CLINIC_NOTIFY_EMAIL below matches your own
# Resend account email — Resend allows sending to yourself even on an
# unverified account.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_your_api_key_here
#
# Where new-booking notifications are sent. On an unverified Resend
# account this MUST be the same email address your Resend account is
# registered under, or sending will fail with a 403.
# CLINIC_NOTIFY_EMAIL=laderma.testing@gmail.com

# --- Once you have a domain ---
# Verify it in Resend's dashboard (Domains → Add Domain), then you can:
# - Set CLINIC_FROM_EMAIL=La Derma <bookings@yourdomain.com> to send
#   the clinic notification from a real address instead of Resend's
#   shared onboarding@resend.dev
# - Optionally move the client confirmation email over to Resend as well,
#   since domain verification removes the "only your own address"
#   restriction that made Brevo necessary in the first place. You could
#   also verify a domain directly in Brevo instead, for better
#   deliverability/branding there.
# CLINIC_FROM_EMAIL=La Derma <bookings@yourdomain.com>

# --- Admin panel (/admin) ---
# Lets staff log in to see all bookings with full client details, cancel
# bookings, and create manual bookings for walk-in clients.
#
# SESSION_SECRET is required for the admin panel to run at all — a long
# random string used to sign login session cookies. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# SESSION_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string
#
# The first admin account is created automatically the first time the
# server starts with no admin accounts yet, using these two variables.
# After that first run, you can change the password or add more staff
# accounts through the admin panel itself — these bootstrap variables are
# only used when the admins table is empty.
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_USERNAME=admin
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=choose-a-strong-password-here
#
# IMPORTANT if hosting on Render's free tier without TURSO_DATABASE_URL
# set (see "Data persistence" above): admin accounts are stored in the
# local-file database, which is wiped on every redeploy, same as the
# Google Calendar token issue. The bootstrap account above will be
# recreated automatically on the next boot after a redeploy, but any
# ADDITIONAL admin accounts you create through the panel itself will be
# lost. Set TURSO_DATABASE_URL (free) to fix this properly.
